.TH "vsearch-sintax" "1" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --sintax \[em] classify sequences using the SINTAX algorithm
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--sintax\f[R] \f[I]fastxfile\f[R] \f[B]--db\f[R]
\f[I]filename\f[R] \f[B]--tabbedout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
[\f[B]--sintax_cutoff\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]] [\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--sintax\f[R] classifies query sequences from a
fasta or fastq file using the SINTAX algorithm (Edgar 2016,
doi:10.1101/074161), a non-Bayesian method for taxonomic classification.
The reference database is specified with \f[V]--db\f[R].
Results are written to \f[V]--tabbedout\f[R].
.PP
Classification works by finding the database sequence with the most
shared k-mers with each query across 100 bootstrap resamples of the
query\[cq]s k-mers.
Each replicate draws 32 of the query\[cq]s \f[I]unique\f[R] k-mers
(queries with fewer than 32 unique k-mers are never classified), and
only counts when the best-matching database sequence shares at least 2
of the drawn k-mers with the query; the taxonomy of that sequence is
then recorded.
A query is classified when at least half of the 100 replicates counted;
the frequency of agreement at each taxonomic rank, divided by the number
of counted replicates, is reported as a bootstrap confidence value.
Unclassified queries produce an output line with empty taxonomy, strand
and cutoff columns.
Use \f[V]--sintax_cutoff\f[R] to filter ranks below a confidence
threshold.
.PP
The reference database must contain taxonomic annotations in the
sequence headers.
Each header must include a \f[V];tax=\f[R] field followed by a
comma-separated list of taxonomic identifiers.
Each identifier starts with a rank letter (\f[V]d\f[R] domain,
\f[V]k\f[R] kingdom, \f[V]p\f[R] phylum, \f[V]c\f[R] class, \f[V]o\f[R]
order, \f[V]f\f[R] family, \f[V]g\f[R] genus, \f[V]s\f[R] species,
\f[V]t\f[R] strain), a colon, and the taxon name.
Commas and semicolons are not allowed in taxon names.
Example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
>X80725;tax=d:Bacteria,p:Proteobacteria,c:Gammaproteobacteria,o:Enterobacteriales,f:Enterobacteriaceae,g:Escherichia,s:Escherichia_coli
ACGT...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The \f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R] option is \f[B]on by default\f[R] for
\f[V]--sintax\f[R], allowing spaces in taxonomic identifiers.
.PP
When ties occur between database sequences with equally many k-mer
matches, the shortest (and then earliest) sequence is chosen by default.
The option \f[V]--sintax_random\f[R] is strongly recommended instead, as
it breaks ties by a random draw and avoids a bias towards shorter
reference sequences.
.PP
For reproducible results, set a fixed seed with \f[V]--randseed\f[R].
The classification is then identical regardless of the number of threads
and reproducible across platforms.
(Output lines are written in processing order, which can vary with
\f[V]--threads\f[R]; sort the output if a fixed line order is also
required.)
.PP
Both strands can be searched with \f[V]--strand both\f[R].
Databases in UDB format are supported (see \f[V]vsearch-udb(5)\f[R]).
This command is multi-threaded.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.TP
\f[V]--db\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Read reference sequences from \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta, fastq, or
UDB format (see \f[V]vsearch-udb(5)\f[R]).
Reference sequence headers must carry taxonomic annotations (see
DESCRIPTION).
This option is mandatory.
\f[V]--db\f[R] accepts \f[V]-\f[R] to read the database from standard
input, as well as an explicit stream path such as \f[V]/dev/stdin\f[R],
a named pipe, or a process substitution.
The query and the database cannot both be \f[V]-\f[R], however, as they
would compete for the same standard input; give at least one of them an
explicit path.
.TP
\f[V]--tabbedout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write classification results to \f[I]filename\f[R] as a tab-separated
file.
Each query produces one row with the following columns:
.RS
.IP "1." 3
query label;
.IP "2." 3
predicted taxonomy with bootstrap confidence in parentheses after each
rank (e.g., \f[V]d:Bacteria(1.00),p:Proteobacteria(0.95)\f[R]);
.IP "3." 3
strand (\f[V]+\f[R] or \f[V]-\f[R]);
.IP "4." 3
predicted taxonomy filtered by \f[V]--sintax_cutoff\f[R], showing only
ranks with bootstrap support at or above the threshold and omitting the
values (e.g., \f[V]d:Bacteria,p:Proteobacteria\f[R]).
Absent if \f[V]--sintax_cutoff\f[R] is not specified.
.RE
.PP
This option is mandatory.
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--dbmask\f[R] \f[I]none\f[R]|\f[I]dust\f[R]|\f[I]soft\f[R]
Mask regions in the database sequences using the \f[I]dust\f[R] method
or the \f[I]soft\f[R] method, or \f[I]none\f[R] to suppress masking.
See \f[V]vsearch-fastx_mask(1)\f[R] for more details.
Warning, when using \f[I]soft\f[R] masking, search commands become case
sensitive: masking excludes masked regions from the \f[I]k\f[R]-mer
index used to select candidate targets (the pairwise alignment itself
always ignores case).
The default is to mask using \f[I]dust\f[R].
Note: \f[V]--sintax\f[R] does not run the DUST algorithm on the
database: masking only controls whether lowercase regions of the
reference sequences are ignored when indexing, so \f[V]dust\f[R] (the
default) behaves like \f[V]soft\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--randseed\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Set the seed for the pseudo-random generator.
A given seed always produces the same results \[em] reproducibly across
platforms and independently of the number of threads \[em] which is
useful for replicability.
By default, vsearch uses a pseudo-random seed if \f[V]--randseed\f[R] is
not set, or set to the special value zero (0).
.TP
\f[V]--sintax_cutoff\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Set the minimum bootstrap support required for a taxonomic rank to be
included in column 4 of the output (e.g., 0.8 for 80%).
Without this option, column 4 is absent.
.TP
\f[V]--sintax_random\f[R]
Break ties between database sequences with equally many k-mer matches by
a random draw, rather than preferring the shortest or earliest sequence
in the database.
This option is strongly recommended as it avoids a bias towards shorter
reference sequences.
.TP
\f[V]--strand\f[R] \f[I]plus\f[R]|\f[I]both\f[R]
Check the \f[I]plus\f[R] strand only (default), or check \f[I]both\f[R]
strands when comparing sequences.
Keywords are case-insensitive.
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the number of computation threads to use, from 1 to 1024.
The number of threads should not exceed the number of available CPU
cores.
The value 0 is also accepted and, like the default, uses all available
cores; decimal values are truncated to their integer part.
On Linux, \[lq]available\[rq] accounts for the CPU affinity mask and the
cgroup CPU quota of the running process, so a job confined by
\f[B]taskset\f[R](1), Slurm, Docker or Kubernetes launches one thread
per core it was actually granted, rather than one per core the machine
has.
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] 33|64
Specify the \f[I]offset\f[R] used as the basis for the fastq quality
score when reading fastq files.
For example, an offset of 33 means that a quality value of 41 is
represented by the 74th ASCII symbol (33 + 41 = 74), which is `J'.
See \f[V]ascii(7)\f[R] for a view of the ASCII character set.
The offset value is either 33 or 64, default is 33.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Option is ignored: quality scores are not checked when reading fastq
sequences, so no score is rejected for being too high.
The argument itself is still validated, as the offset (see
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the maximal score may not exceed 126, the
last printable ASCII character.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Option is ignored: quality scores are not checked when reading fastq
sequences, so no score is rejected for being too low.
The argument itself is still validated, as the offset (see
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the minimal score must be at least 33, the
first printable ASCII character.
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--label_suffix\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the suffix \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers when writing fasta
or fastq files.
For example, with \f[V]--label_suffix \[dq];status=healthy\[dq]\f[R],
sequence header `>seq1' becomes `>seq1;status=healthy'.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
.TP
\f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard database sequences longer than \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
(50,000 nucleotides by default).
.TP
\f[V]--minseqlength\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard database sequences shorter than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] (32
nucleotides by default).
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R]
Retain whole sequence headers in output files.
By default, vsearch truncates sequence headers at first space or
tabulation.
This option suppresses truncation.
Always implied with \f[V]--sintax\f[R] (headers are never truncated, so
that taxonomy annotations may contain spaces); it cannot be disabled.
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--wordlength\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the length of words (i.e.\ \f[I]k\f[R]-mers) used for sequence
indexing and comparisons.
Valid values range from 3 to 15.
The default is 8.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Classify query sequences against a taxonomy-annotated reference
database, writing all confidence values to the output:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --sintax queries.fasta \[rs]
    --db reference.fasta \[rs]
    --tabbedout classification.tsv
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Classify and report only ranks with at least 80% bootstrap support
(\f[V]--sintax_cutoff 0.8\f[R]), using the recommended tie-breaking
option and searching both strands:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --sintax queries.fasta \[rs]
    --db reference.fasta \[rs]
    --sintax_cutoff 0.8 \[rs]
    --sintax_random \[rs]
    --strand both \[rs]
    --tabbedout classification.tsv
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Produce reproducible results using a fixed random seed:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --sintax queries.fasta \[rs]
    --db reference.fasta \[rs]
    --sintax_cutoff 0.8 \[rs]
    --sintax_random \[rs]
    --randseed 42 \[rs]
    --tabbedout classification.tsv
\f[R]
.fi
